  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe tackle the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by formulating it as a constrained optimization problem with explicit handling of the integral constraint and boundary conditions. Unlike previous approaches relying on stochastic local search, our method combines mathematical structure and deterministic optimization:\n\nThe central idea is to represent the step function through Fourier projection modes, enabling global exploration while maintaining analytical control. This approach has three key advantages:\n    - Fourier basis naturally captures periodic symmetry relevant to overlap minimization\n    - Factorization of high-dimensional variables into coefficient space enables efficient optimization\n    - Mode-dependent amplitude constraints prevent boundary violations\n\nHowever, this approach has limitations:\n    1. Discrete sampling may induce aliasing artifacts\n    2. Gradients may be poorly conditioned\n    3. Setup costs for large n require regularization heuristics\n\nWe use projected gradient descent with exponential smoothing to approximate the max-constraint. The integral is ensured by a dual constraint set with subgradient projection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n**Variables**:\n- Fourier series coefficients {a_k, b_k, c_k} \u2208 \u211d\n- Integration scale factor s \u2208 [0.5, 1.5]\n- Lag position k \u2208 [0, 2n-2]\n\n**Objective**:\n   C(n) = (2/n) * max_{k} \u222b_{-\u221e}^\u221e h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n   \u2248 (2/n) * max_{k} \u2211_i h[i] (1-h[i+k])  [with padding for boundary]\n\n**Constraints**:\n   1. Boundary: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   2. Integral: \u2211_{i} h[i] * dx = n * 0.5 * dx = 0.5 (since dx=2/n)\n\nThe optimization surface is non-convex but continuous where defined.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n3.1. Initialization (linear in n)\n- Input validation: If parent exists, validate length is divisible by 8\n- Generate candidate set through three transformations:\n   a. Four-parameter Fourier fit: \n        h_base(x) = 0.5 + 0.8 * \u2211_{m=1}^M [a_m sin(2\u03c0 f_m x) + b_m cos(2\u03c0 g_m x)]\n   b. Adjustment for integral with s (scaler)\n   c. Clipping and normalization to unit integral\n\n3.2. Fourier transformation\n- Use DFT to generate samples:\n   for i in range(n): x[i] = i * 2.0 / n\n   h_base[i] = FourierSeries(x, n, n)[i]\n\n3.3. Projection to valid domain:\n- Reshape to explicit samples: h_full[i] = clip(h_base[i], 0, 1)\n- Adjust for integral and boundary:\n\n3.4. Optimization:\n- Use coordinate descent between harmonic frequencies\n- For each frequency set:\n   - Apply exponential smoothing to correlation targets\n   - Use projected gradient with:\n        Gradient = \u2202C/\u2202a_k \u2248 finite differences (forward/backward)\n        Projection = max(0, min(1, h_full[i]))\n        Integral adjustment via scaled L2 projection\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nSupported varieties:\n- Variance scaling: log-uniform scaling of coefficients [0.1, 1.0] \n   - Default: uniform random sampling\n   - Optimize when average decrease > 0.001 per iteration\n\n- Mode structure:\n   - Harmonic progression: frequencies (f, 2f, 3f, 4f) with f=golden ratio (0.618...)\n   - Alternate: wavelet-inspired frequency progression\n\n- Regularization: l2 weight penalty (\u03bb=0.01 by default)\n   - Decreasing \u03bb as function of iterations until convergence\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n5.1. Time estimation:\n- Mode-wise optimization time: O(m * log(iterations)) with m=number of modes\n- Critical step: Initial setup O(n log n)\n\n5.2. Resource allocation:\n   - First 20% budget: Initialization + detection of candidate patterns\n   - Next 70% budget: Progressive mode refinement\n   - Final 10% budget: Clustering and mode pruning\n\n5.3. Failure handling:\n   - Early termination if mode energy > 0.99 * max_energy\n   - Restart from parent solution if cross-over error > 0.3%\n   - Local repair through targeted mode dampening\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n6.1. Feasibility checks:\n   a. Integral balance: |sum(h) - 0.5*n| \u2264 1e-5\n   b. Boundary checks: all(0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1)\n   c. Pattern consistency: time-reversibility with target error < 0.1%\n\n6.2. Objective recomputation:\n   - Use a five-point moving average of the correlation across lags\n   - Double-check with a coarser grid if discrepancy > 1e-4 relative\n\n6.3. Fallback structure:\n   - Retain parent solution if gradient vanishes (||\u2207C|| < 1e-5)\n   - Fallback solution: Sample from initial Fourier coefficients\n   - Safety timeout: exit\u8129opt iteration > 15%",
